    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 66 tests pass, including the new `Cp54Tests`. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` feature: an owner's `capacityWarning` is returned `true` when their city already holds between 40 and 49 owners (inclusive) at create time \u2014 approaching but not reaching the hard limit of 50. The existing hard rejection at 50 (`CityAtCapacityException`, 409) is unchanged.\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added `assignCapacityWarning(owner)` to the create flow. It sets the flag `true` when `count >= 40 && count < 50`. Extracted the shared, case-insensitive per-city counting into a `countOwnersInCity(city)` helper now used by both the warning and the existing `rejectCityAtCapacity` hard check, keeping the two counts consistent.\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `capacityWarning` field (mapped to `capacity_warning`) with getter/setter, so MapStruct maps it to the DTO by name (same pattern as `bulkSignupWarning`).\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema (regenerates `OwnerDto`).\n- **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 Added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column. Tests run under the `hsqldb` profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema column is required.\n\nThe counting is taken over existing owners before the new one is persisted (matching `rejectCityAtCapacity` and the other warning rules), so a fresh city filled to 40 warns on the 41st create \u2014 exactly what the spec and `Cp54Tests` require.",
